%% ABSTRACT-BEGIN
\begin{abstract}
Optically pumped magnetometers (OPMs) worn on the scalp sit closer to the brain than the sensors of cryogenic magnetoencephalography (MEG), but whether they detect cortical sources better depends on noise, which spherical-head analyses reduce to an assumed ratio. Here, we compared the << F.g2_squid_channels >>-channel Neuromag system with two single-axis OPM arrays (<< F.g2_dense_sites >> dense sites; Neuromag's << F.g2_matched_sites >> sensor sites) by matched-filter detectability with explicit sensor, brain and room noise, in an adult and in smaller heads in the adult helmet or a helmet fitted to the adult's gap. In the adult, the dense array's detectability was << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> times Neuromag's (95 % parcel-bootstrap interval << F.g2_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >>) up to a break-even OPM noise of << F.rev_ns_breakeven_dense_vs_combined_ib_asd >>~fT/√Hz; the site-matched array broke even at << F.rev_ns_breakeven_matched_vs_combined_ib_asd >>~fT/√Hz. With Neuromag's measured noise covariance, the dense ratio was << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ci >>, so measured OPM noise must decide the adult comparison. In << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled copies of the adult and << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates, the fixed adult helmet added << F.g3b_templates_scaled_dense_vs_combined_ib_delta_range >>~dB to the OPM advantage, and fitting the helmet removed << F.wr_cgap_scaled_templates_interaction_range >>~dB of this excess, a contrast that depends on the SQUID helmet alone. Under the modeled noise and with an idealized detector, the dense array detected simulated superficial interictal spikes at << F.cf_all_dense_vs_combined_practical_reduction_pct_range >> lower strength than Neuromag in every head, without a localization difference. Three school-aged children with unverified near-scalp anatomy gave provisional results. In this model, array design, OPM noise and the fit of the SQUID helmet, not proximity alone, determine the on-scalp advantage.
\end{abstract}
