\section{Materials and methods}\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Study design and estimands}\label{sec:design}

We compared arrays by known-topography detectability, the output SNR of the matched filter for a source of known field pattern in noise of known covariance:
\begin{equation}
d = \sqrt{\symbf{s}^{\top}\symbf{C}^{-1}\symbf{s}},
\label{eq:d}
\end{equation}
where $\symbf{s}$ is the noise-free field of a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm cortical dipole at the array's channels and $\symbf{C}$ is the array's noise covariance (Section~\ref{sec:noise}), taken as known (the oracle covariance). For each target, two arrays were compared by the ratio $d_{\mathrm{OPM}}/d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}$ or, in decibels, by
\begin{equation}
D = 20\log_{10}\frac{d_{\mathrm{OPM}}}{d_{\mathrm{Neuromag}}},
\label{eq:D}
\end{equation}
which is positive when the OPM array detects the source better.

For the adult, we summarize the target-wise ratios by their unweighted median over all << F.g2_n_targets >> targets, including the << F.g2_medialwall_n >> on the medial wall, which is not cortex; this is the adult analysis's declared convention, and without them the dense array's ratio is << F.dp("wr_g2_dense_ib_ratio_no_medial_wall", 2) >>. The smaller heads differ in cortical area, so for each of them we summarize $D$ by its area-weighted median over cortical targets, medial wall excluded. A smaller head's change from the adult, $\Delta$, is the area-weighted median of paired target-level differences in $D$, with the adult placed by the same helmet rule; it is not the difference of the two heads' medians. Targets are paired per vertex for the scaled adults, which share the adult's vertices, and per atlas parcel for the other heads. Intervals are << F.g2_ci_level_pct >> percentile bootstrap intervals over atlas parcels (Section~\ref{sec:stats}).

\subsection{Head models}\label{sec:heads}

The adult head was the MNE sample subject \citep{gramfort2013}, with FreeSurfer surfaces and atlas labels \citep{desikan2006,fischl2012}. Targets were the vertices of an MNE-Python oct-6 source space (a recursively subdivided octahedron) on the white-matter surface lying at least << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm inside the inner skull, each a << F.g2_focal_nam >>-nAm dipole normal to the cortex. A target's depth was its distance to the nearest point of the MRI scalp. A three-layer boundary-element model (BEM; \citealp{hamalainen1989}) with conductivities of << F.g2_conductivity >>~S/m had its outer surface on the MRI scalp.

The << F.wr_n_smaller_heads_words >> smaller heads fell into three classes (Table~\ref{tab:anatomies}), which carry different weight. The << F.wr_n_scaled_words >> scaled adults are copies of the adult scaled to school-age size and to the head circumference of the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>-month template; they keep the adult's anatomy exactly and carry the comparison of head size and helmet fit. The << F.wr_n_templates_words >> infant templates, at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months, are averages of many MRIs \citep{oreilly2021,richards2016}; they add infant head shapes, with the qualifications given below. The << F.wr_n_children_words >> typically developing school-aged children, A, B and C (<< F.g3b_children_age_range >> years), came from OpenNeuro dataset ds005234 \citep{fadeev2024,fadeev2025} and are reported as provisional examples. The anatomies are therefore not independent replications, and all of them share the noise calibration, the adult's conductivities and the processing.

The children's anatomy needed three further steps (Section~S4). First, the dataset's BEM surfaces placed the inner skull a median of << F.g3b_children_watershed_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >>~mm below the scalp over the upper head, implausibly close. We moved it inward toward << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_depth >>~mm below the scalp wherever it lay shallower, keeping every inner-skull vertex at least << F.g3b_cfg_child_skull_min_cortex >>~mm from the white-surface vertices, so that over the upper head it lay a median of << F.g3b_children_modelled_inner_skull_depth_p50_range >>~mm below the scalp (adult, << F.g3b_adult_inner_skull_depth_p50 >>~mm). Between vertices, the inner skull came within << F.g3b_children_white_to_inner_skull_closest_range >>~mm of the white surface, and in child B the outer skull came within << F.g3b_child_b_outer_skull_to_head_thinnest_2dp >>~mm of the scalp. A single-compartment BEM, which needs no skull, changed the difference between each smaller head's median $D$ and the adult's by << F.meth_bem1_change_db_range >>~dB.

Second, no fiducials come with these data. We transferred them from the adult by a similarity transform fitted between the adult's and the child's white surfaces (trimmed iterative closest points) and moved each to the nearest scalp vertex. Applied to the infant templates, this method reproduced their own fiducials with errors of << F.g3b_templates_fid_transfer_range >>~mm and their head frames with rotations of << F.g3b_templates_fid_transfer_rotation_range >>°. Third, because the children's white surfaces came within << F.rev_qc_children_white_scalp_min_mm_range >>~mm of the scalp, we checked the surfaces against their T1-weighted MRIs (Fig.~S6). The FreeSurfer scalp lay about as far inside the T1 head boundary in the children (median << F.wr_qc_children_offset_inside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm) as in the adult (<< F.wr_qc_adult_offset_inside_otsu_mm >>~mm), and the white surfaces were registered to the T1 images. Relative to these images, the shallow cortex is therefore not a scalp-placement or registration error.

The count of shallow targets depends on the scalp definition: measured to the MRI head boundary, child B has << F.wr_qc_child_b_targets_lt10mm_mri_n >> rather than << F.g3b_child_b_shallow_targets_lt10mm >> targets less than 10~mm deep. The source rule left out << F.wr_excl_children_pct_range >> of the children's cortex (adult, << F.wr_excl_adult_pct >>), including << F.wr_excl_children_lt8mm_pct_range >> of their cortex within 8~mm of the scalp. The infant templates' scalps lay << F.wr_qc_templates_offset_outside_otsu_mm_range >>~mm outside their own MRI head boundaries, whereas the adult's lies inside. The check also classed the << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month template as << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_verdict >>: its white surface fitted its T1 image best after a << F.wr_qc_infant18mo_white_best_shift_mm >>-mm shift (<< F.wr_qc_templates_other_white_best_shift_mm >>~mm in the other two templates).

