\subsection{The noise model against measured Neuromag noise}\label{sec:res-noise}

The model predicted << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the magnetometers' measured brain-noise amplitude, an independent prediction (Fig.~S2), or << F.rev_cov_heart_mag_amp_without_ratio >> with the heart's field removed. The gradiometer level at randomly held-out sites (<< F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >>; << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >> over << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> splits) and the overall pattern of channel correlations agreed with the measurement, the former nearly by construction. The modeled brain noise was, however, too flat from inferior to superior sites (<< F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >> and << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >> when fitted on the inferior or the superior half), and its magnetometer correlations and per-channel variances were poorly predicted.

The measured covariance thus differs from the modeled one in structure as well as in level: the magnetometers' excess over the model lay in a few far-field components (Section~S3). With its measured covariance, Neuromag's detectability was << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_det_combined_corrected_ci >> times its modeled value (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}A), a factor as large as the dense array's whole advantage. What this implies for the OPM depends on its real noise, which no measurement here constrains (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}B).

If the model's error was common to both systems (scenario A), the ratios remained << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s1_ratio >> and << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s1_ratio >>. They remained close to these values when both cortical backgrounds were scaled by the magnetometers' variance excess over the model, a factor of << F.rev_cov_k_ratio >> (scenario B), or when Neuromag's measured empty-room noise was combined with the modeled brain noise (scenario E). With Neuromag taken as measured and the OPM as modeled (scenario D), the dense ratio was << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s4_ci >> and the site-matched ratio << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s4_ratio >>. With the OPM bearing the whole magnetometer variance excess as cortical noise (scenario C, the most pessimistic), the ratios were << F.rev_cov_opm_dense_vs_combined_s3_ratio >> and << F.rev_cov_opm_matched_vs_combined_s3_ratio >>.

The omissions we could test moved the advantage little at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz (Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}C; Table~S4). The cortex << F.rev_ns_joint_floor_mm >>--<< F.rev_ns_near_rule_mm >>~mm from the inner skull gave << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_bg_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> when added as background and << F.rev_ns_near_primary_f2mm_targets_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> when added as targets. A 1/$f$ component with a << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner gave << F.rev_ns_col_bv_asd15ft_c10hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> under the primary detector and << F.rev_ns_col_fr_asd15ft_c10hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> under one that also whitens across frequency, and cardiac and ocular sources changed the dense ratio by << F.rev_ns_ff_dense_vs_combined_ib_change_pct_range >>. With near-skull background and the << F.rev_ns_joint_corner_hz >>-Hz corner together and frequency whitening (the joint run; Fig.~\ref{fig:noise}D, dashed), the ratio was << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd15ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz, about 1 at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>) and << F.rev_ns_joint_nofar_asd30ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_max >>~fT/√Hz. The primary model gave << F.g2_sens_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> << F.g2_sens_asd20ft_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci >> at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz.

